---
// New Explore-only story: "AI answered you. How did it know what to say?" —
// Card 2 of the four homepage preview cards. Scene 1 only, on exactly the
// construction /explore/looks-real-is-it, /explore/where-is-the-5g and the
// photo story all use: one grid, copy and the interaction on the left, the
// visual stage on the right; the left title stays the anchor for the whole
// story once later scenes exist, the same way none of the other three
// stories' opening headings change once their own story has started.
//
// The supplied Card2 render is an ordinary chat exchange — a question asked,
// an answer given — with nothing else drawn over it: no glow, no
// neural-network graphic, no floating tokens, no model diagram, no
// technical label. That ordinariness is the whole point of this opening:
// you asked, AI answered, in seconds — the "how" is what the story past
// this point exists to answer, not something this scene explains in
// advance.
//
// Scene 2 (whatever "How?" actually reveals) does not exist yet.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI answered you. How did it know what to say? — DekhoYahan"
  description="In seconds."
>
  <div class="answer" data-answer data-stage="1">
    <!-- Same reasoning as the other three openings: copy and the visual
         share one grid rather than a header sitting above a separate
         stage, so on wide screens they sit side by side and both the
         question and the CTA are readable before the visitor scrolls past
         the image; below the stacking point they fall back to the same
         top-to-bottom order, copy first.

         Carries the same small eyebrow the "Looks real. Is it?" opening
         uses — reused as-is (small saffron dot, cobalt uppercase) rather
         than inventing a new treatment — since this heading is also a
         single short line rather than a two-part statement-and-question,
         so the eyebrow is what frames it as this story's own prompt
         ("you asked") rather than a flat statement. -->
    <div class="answer__stage shell">
      <div class="answer__copy">
        <p class="answer__eyebrow"><span></span>You asked</p>

        <h1 class="answer__opening">AI answered you.</h1>

        <p class="answer__noticed">In seconds.</p>

        <!-- The one thing that starts the story. Real and focusable now,
             the same way every other story's opening control is, even
             though nothing past it exists yet. -->
        <button type="button" class="answer__show" data-show>
          How? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <!-- ====================================================
           THE VISUALS — Scene 1's photo, then Scene 2's sentence taking
           its place entirely rather than sitting beside or behind it. The
           two states don't share a visual language (a locked photo, then
           plain typography) so there's no crossfade to stage between
           them — one simply stops occupying the column as the other
           starts, the same on/off handoff every other Explore story uses
           between a beat's own kicker/text pair and the one that replaces
           it. -->
      <div class="answer__visuals">
        <!-- THE SCENE — the supplied Card2 render, used as-is and locked.

             A plain photograph of an ordinary moment rather than a
             composed technical illustration, so its box is simply sized
             to its own ratio (1536 x 1024, 3:2) with no overlay, effect,
             label or AI indicator drawn over it — and stays exactly that
             way for as long as it's on screen. -->
        <div class="answer__scene">
          <img
            class="answer__art"
            src="/images/Card2.webp"
            alt="A person holding a phone, reading an AI assistant's reply to the question 'Why do we dream?' — the answer already on screen."
            width="1536"
            height="1024"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>

        <!-- ====================================================
             SCENE 2 — the plainest possible next-word moment: a sentence
             with a gap, and the three candidates for it, set as running
             type rather than as buttons or cards so nothing here reads as
             an interface to operate. "mat" is simply marked as the word
             this beat leans on (.answer__word--mat) — the CSS below is
             what makes that lean visible, on a delay, with no click or
             script involved. Nothing past that lean is explained yet:
             just the one line naming what just happened, and the way
             onward. -->
        <div class="answer__sentence">
          <p class="answer__sentence-text">
            The cat sat on the <span class="answer__blank">___</span>
          </p>

          <p class="answer__words">
            <span class="answer__word answer__word--mat">mat</span>
            <span class="answer__word">moon</span>
            <span class="answer__word">democracy</span>
          </p>

          <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">Some words feel more likely to come next.</p>

          <!-- The way onward. -->
          <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-change>
            Change the sentence <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </button>

          <!-- ====================================================
               SCENE 3 — the same plain mechanism run again, on purpose:
               same sentence shape, same three running words, same lean
               after a beat, same one-line caption and turn. Nothing new
               is drawn or explained — the point of repeating the exact
               idiom is to let the visitor feel, without being told, that
               it's the same simple thing happening twice. Only the words
               changed, and which one the lean falls on changed with them
               ("moon" leads the list this time and is what leans, not
               "mat") — that's the whole demonstration: change the words
               before the gap, and the word that feels right for it
               changes too.

               Nested one level inside .answer__sentence so the child
               combinator below — which reaches only .answer__sentence's
               own direct children — can hide Scene 2's sentence, words,
               kicker and turn without also catching this one, the same
               hand-over technique every other beat in this beat's sibling
               stories already uses. -->
          <div class="answer__sentence2">
            <p class="answer__sentence-text">
              The astronaut sat on the <span class="answer__blank">___</span>
            </p>

            <p class="answer__words">
              <span class="answer__word answer__word--moon">moon</span>
              <span class="answer__word">mat</span>
              <span class="answer__word">democracy</span>
            </p>

            <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">
              Change the words before it, and what comes next can change.
            </p>

            <!-- The way onward. -->
            <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-choose>
              So how does AI choose? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </button>

            <!-- ====================================================
                 SCENE 4 — the same three words, now shown unevenly rather
                 than evenly: a simple typographic ranking, not a chart. No
                 axis, no legend, no percentage — just bars set as running
                 characters, in the same row the words themselves sit in,
                 so this still reads as part of the sentence rather than a
                 widget dropped beside it.

                 ".answer__sentence-text" (the astronaut sentence itself)
                 is deliberately left out of what this hand-over hides —
                 unlike every earlier beat, the sentence doesn't change or
                 refade here, only the row under it does, which is what
                 keeps this feeling like the same moment continuing rather
                 than a new one starting. Nested one level inside
                 .answer__sentence2, same technique as every hand-over
                 before it. -->
            <div class="answer__sentence3">
              <ul class="answer__ranking">
                <li class="answer__rank">
                  <span class="answer__rank-word answer__rank-word--lead">moon</span>
                  <span class="answer__rank-bar" aria-hidden="true">█████████</span>
                  <span class="answer__sr-only">most likely</span>
                </li>
                <li class="answer__rank">
                  <span class="answer__rank-word">mat</span>
                  <span class="answer__rank-bar" aria-hidden="true">██</span>
                  <span class="answer__sr-only">less likely</span>
                </li>
                <li class="answer__rank">
                  <span class="answer__rank-word">democracy</span>
                  <span class="answer__rank-bar answer__rank-bar--faint" aria-hidden="true">▏</span>
                  <span class="answer__sr-only">barely likely</span>
                </li>
              </ul>

              <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">AI gives possible next words different chances.</p>
              <p class="answer__reveal-text">One word is chosen. Then the next.</p>

              <!-- The way onward. -->
              <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-again>
                Watch it continue <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </button>

              <!-- ====================================================
                   SCENE 5 — the bars are gone; the sentence itself is the
                   only visual left. "The astronaut sat on the moon" is
                   the piece already settled (Scene 4's own choice), set
                   down all at once with the rest of this block; everything
                   after it — "and", "looked", "back", "at", "Earth." —
                   is exactly the piece-by-piece mechanism Scenes 2-4 spent
                   three beats making felt, just no longer interrupted by a
                   caption or a click between one word and the next. Each
                   span carries its own entrance delay below, spaced far
                   enough apart to read as a deliberate pause rather than a
                   typing effect — plain opacity/position, the same
                   `answer-in` keyframe every reveal on this page already
                   uses, never a cursor, a clip-width reveal or anything
                   else that would read as a terminal typing itself out.

                   This hand-over hides .answer__sentence2's own
                   `.answer__sentence-text` too, not just .answer__sentence3's
                   ranking/kicker/text/turn — the astronaut sentence is
                   changing for the first time since Scene 3 set it, so
                   this is the one beat since then where the old sentence
                   actually needs to leave rather than persist. -->
              <div class="answer__sentence4">
                <p class="answer__sentence-text answer__built">
                  <span class="answer__piece answer__piece--base">The astronaut sat on the moon</span>
                  <span class="answer__piece" data-piece="1">and</span>
                  <span class="answer__piece" data-piece="2">looked</span>
                  <span class="answer__piece" data-piece="3">back</span>
                  <span class="answer__piece" data-piece="4">at</span>
                  <span class="answer__piece" data-piece="5">Earth.</span>
                </p>

                <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">One choice becomes context for the next.</p>
                <p class="answer__reveal-text">Again. And again.</p>

                <!-- The way onward. -->
                <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-answer-final>
                  Until you get an answer <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                </button>

                <!-- ====================================================
                     SCENE 6 — the built sentence stays exactly as Scene 5
                     left it (".answer__built" is left out of what this
                     hand-over hides, the same way ".answer__sentence-text"
                     stayed through Scene 4); everything new sits beneath
                     it rather than replacing it.

                     Three examples, not one, so the range itself is what
                     explains the idea rather than a single word standing
                     in for every case. All three are real GPT-4
                     (cl100k_base) output, checked directly with tiktoken
                     rather than guessed at — this is one real tokenizer's
                     own boundaries, not a universal rule every model
                     shares, which is exactly why three different shapes
                     are shown instead of a single definition: "cat" is
                     its own one piece, "unbelievable" is three, "Hello!"
                     is two, with the "!" a piece entirely on its own.

                     Each multi-piece example carries the same
                     zero-width/zero-opacity "·" marks Scene 6 used
                     before: not just invisible before they widen in, but
                     genuinely taking up no space, so each word still
                     reads as one solid unit first and then visibly opens
                     — the same dictionary-syllabification mark, never a
                     chip, pill or code token. "cat" has no mark at all,
                     since it has nothing to open — that contrast (two
                     words visibly splitting, one that simply doesn't) is
                     what actually carries "a token can be a whole word
                     too" without a line of copy saying so. The whole
                     three-line demo is aria-hidden with each example's
                     clean text given back via aria-label, since the
                     surrounding sentences already say in words everything
                     it demonstrates. -->
                <div class="answer__sentence5">
                  <p class="answer__reveal-kicker answer__reveal-kicker--1">
                    AI doesn't always see whole words.
                  </p>

                  <div class="answer__tokens">
                    <p class="answer__token answer__token--whole" aria-label="cat">
                      <span class="answer__token-piece">cat</span>
                    </p>

                    <p class="answer__token answer__token--word" aria-label="unbelievable">
                      <span class="answer__token-piece">un</span><span
                        class="answer__token-sep"
                        aria-hidden="true">·</span><span class="answer__token-piece"
                        >belie</span><span class="answer__token-sep" aria-hidden="true"
                        >·</span><span class="answer__token-piece">vable</span>
                    </p>

                    <p class="answer__token answer__token--punct" aria-label="Hello!">
                      <span class="answer__token-piece">Hello</span><span
                        class="answer__token-sep"
                        aria-hidden="true">·</span><span class="answer__token-piece">!</span>
                    </p>
                  </div>

                  <p class="answer__reveal-kicker answer__reveal-kicker--2">
                    It works with pieces called tokens.
                  </p>
                  <p class="answer__reveal-text">
                    A token might be a word, part of one, or even punctuation.
                  </p>

                  <!-- The way onward. -->
                  <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-why>
                    Why pieces? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                  </button>

                  <!-- Scene 7: where the guess came from. Nested one level
                       inside .answer__sentence5, same hand-over technique
                       as every earlier beat. The field is eighteen ordinary
                       fragments — the kind of sentence you'd meet in a
                       book, an email, a forum thread, a caption — never a
                       logo, a screenshot or a database icon. Six of those
                       fragments carry the three paired words this beat
                       cares about (astronaut/space, moon/Earth, cat/mat),
                       written in place exactly like their neighbours. The
                       field itself never changes; only which words wear
                       emphasis does, arriving together in the three pairs
                       — astronaut with space, moon with Earth, cat with
                       mat — so the pattern seems to surface from inside
                       the ordinary language already on screen rather than
                       being pointed at from outside it. The field is
                       aria-hidden with a single sr-only line standing in
                       for it, since what it demonstrates is already said
                       in words by the copy around it. -->
                  <div class="answer__sentence6">
                    <p class="answer__sentence-text">
                      How did AI know "moon" was more likely?
                    </p>

                    <!-- Scene 8 stacks .answer__values directly on top of
                         .answer__field (same grid cell, see the CSS below),
                         so the numbers arrive in the exact footprint the
                         example text occupied rather than somewhere new.
                         The text never leaves the flow — it fades by colour
                         alone — so the area keeps its height and the values
                         land across the space the sentences were holding.
                         Wrapping the two in one stage element is the only
                         markup change Scene 8 makes to Scene 7's own
                         field. -->
                    <div class="answer__field-stage">
                      <div class="answer__field" aria-hidden="true">
                        <span class="answer__frag">an old photograph from the archive</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="1">astronaut</span> drifted toward the horizon</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">documentation for the new API</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">she left a comment on the thread</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a museum plaque explaining the exhibit</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">notes from a lecture on optics</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a recipe passed down for generations</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the mission control room fell quiet</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">further reading on the topic</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag"><span class="answer__pair" data-beat="1">space</span> debris drifted past the window</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a letter never sent</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="2">astronaut</span> checked the equipment twice</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">reviews from readers around the world</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="2">moon</span> rose over the horizon that night</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a forum thread about gardening</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="3">moon</span>'s surface caught the light</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">an email from a colleague</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="3">Earth</span> turned slowly beneath them</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">footnotes in an old textbook</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a shopping list stuck to the fridge</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">the <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="4">moon</span> completed another slow pass</span>
                        <span class="answer__frag">a satellite settled into a steady <span class="answer__pair" data-beat="4">orbit</span></span>
                      </div>

                      <!-- What the training left behind. Loosely set rather
                           than ruled into rows and columns — each value is
                           nudged off the line its neighbours sit on, so the
                           field reads as scattered quantities rather than a
                           spreadsheet, a matrix or a block of code. Set in
                           the page's own sans at reading size, never a
                           monospace/terminal face, and never boxed, ruled
                           or glowing. -->
                      <div class="answer__values" aria-hidden="true">
                        <span class="answer__value">0.18</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.42</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.07</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.63</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.21</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.96</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.73</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.34</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.28</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−1.31</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.25</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.82</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.56</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.14</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.37</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.41</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.92</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.68</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.33</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.15</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.22</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.71</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−1.05</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.47</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.19</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.64</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.93</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.28</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.55</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.01</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.39</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.29</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.83</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−1.22</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.61</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.37</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.81</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.24</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.17</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.88</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.33</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.77</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−1.16</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.49</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">0.26</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.58</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">1.09</span>
                        <span class="answer__value">−0.24</span>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="answer__sr-only">
                      A field of ordinary text fragments — from books, articles, conversations
                      and documentation — in which certain words keep recurring near each
                      other: astronaut near space, astronaut near moon, moon near Earth, and
                      moon near orbit.
                    </p>

                    <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">It learned patterns from enormous amounts of text.</p>
                    <p class="answer__reveal-text">
                      Which words and ideas tend to appear together — and in what contexts.
                    </p>

                    <!-- The way onward. -->
                    <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-learn>
                      But learning isn't remembering <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                    </button>

                    <!-- Scene 8: the examples are gone, something changed.
                         Nested one level inside .answer__sentence6, the
                         same hand-over every earlier beat uses — except
                         .answer__field is deliberately left out of what
                         this hand-over hides (alongside
                         .answer__sentence-text, which has carried over
                         since Scene 6), because the fading text and the
                         points it leaves behind ARE this scene's opening
                         beat, not something to clear before it starts. Only
                         Scene 7's own kicker/text/turn — the "it learned
                         patterns" line and its CTA, now said and done —
                         are hidden. -->
                    <div class="answer__sentence7">
                      <p class="answer__sr-only">
                        The example text fades away. What remains in its place is a
                        loose field of small numerical values — not the sentences
                        themselves, but what training left behind.
                      </p>

                      <!-- The turn of the whole beat, and the only place this
                           scene raises its voice: the two halves are set in the
                           story's own serif, the second in cobalt, so "gone" and
                           "remain" land as one statement rather than a label and
                           a caption. -->
                      <p class="answer__verdict">
                        <span class="answer__verdict-gone">The sentences are gone.</span>
                        <span class="answer__verdict-remain">The changes remain.</span>
                      </p>

                      <p class="answer__reveal-text answer__reveal-text--support">
                        Those changes are carried in billions of numerical values.
                      </p>
                      <p class="answer__reveal-text answer__reveal-text--term">
                        These are called <strong>parameters</strong>.
                      </p>

                      <!-- The way onward. -->
                      <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-become>
                        So how does that become an answer? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                      </button>

                      <!-- ====================================================
                           SCENE 9 — the question comes back.
                           ====================================================
                           The beat the whole story has been walking toward: the
                           mechanism watched in the abstract earlier (Scenes 2–5,
                           where a sentence chose its own next word) is now shown
                           running on the reader's own question. Nothing new is
                           explained — the ranking is deliberately the SAME
                           .answer__ranking / .answer__rank markup Scene 4 used,
                           down to the block-character bars, so the recognition
                           lands as "oh — that was this" rather than as a second
                           mechanism being introduced.

                           The chosen token is appended to the question itself
                           rather than restated on a line of its own: the prompt
                           visibly becomes the thing being continued, which is
                           the point of the beat, and it echoes Scene 5's own
                           word-by-word construction without repeating it.

                           Nested one level inside .answer__sentence7, same
                           hand-over technique as every beat before it. -->
                      <div class="answer__sentence8">
                        <p class="answer__prompt">
                          <span class="answer__prompt-q">Why do we dream?</span><span
                            class="answer__prompt-next"
                          >&nbsp;Scientists</span><span class="answer__prompt-more" aria-hidden="true">&nbsp;…</span><span
                            class="answer__grow"
                            data-grow-piece="1"
                          >&nbsp;still</span><span class="answer__grow" data-grow-piece="2"
                          >&nbsp;don't</span><span class="answer__grow" data-grow-piece="3"
                          >&nbsp;fully understand</span><span class="answer__grow" data-grow-piece="4"
                          >&nbsp;why we dream.</span>
                        </p>

                        <ul class="answer__ranking answer__ranking--prompt">
                          <li class="answer__rank">
                            <span class="answer__rank-word answer__rank-word--pick">Scientists</span>
                            <span class="answer__rank-bar" aria-hidden="true">█████████</span>
                            <span class="answer__sr-only">most likely</span>
                          </li>
                          <li class="answer__rank">
                            <span class="answer__rank-word">Dreams</span>
                            <span class="answer__rank-bar" aria-hidden="true">█████</span>
                            <span class="answer__sr-only">less likely</span>
                          </li>
                          <li class="answer__rank">
                            <span class="answer__rank-word">There</span>
                            <span class="answer__rank-bar" aria-hidden="true">██</span>
                            <span class="answer__sr-only">less likely still</span>
                          </li>
                          <li class="answer__rank">
                            <span class="answer__rank-word">We</span>
                            <span class="answer__rank-bar answer__rank-bar--faint" aria-hidden="true">▏</span>
                            <span class="answer__sr-only">barely likely</span>
                          </li>
                        </ul>

                        <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">Your question becomes the context.</p>
                        <p class="answer__reveal-text">
                          Using what it learned, the model gives possible next tokens
                          different chances.
                        </p>

                        <!-- The way onward. -->
                        <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-next>
                          And then? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                        </button>

                        <!-- ====================================================
                             SCENE 10 — the answer takes shape.
                             ====================================================
                             The payoff, not another explanation. The candidate
                             list and its bars go; the sentence is left alone with
                             the column and simply continues, one piece at a time,
                             each arriving in cobalt and settling to ink a moment
                             later so the reader can watch the answer being built
                             rather than being told about it. "Scientists" — the
                             piece chosen in the previous beat, still standing in
                             cobalt — settles the same way as the next piece
                             arrives, which is what makes the loop legible: what
                             was just generated becomes ordinary context, and the
                             next piece is generated against it.

                             The pieces themselves live inside .answer__prompt
                             (declared there, display:none until this beat) so the
                             sentence grows in place rather than being restated.
                             Whole pieces, never characters — the conceptual unit
                             is the token, and a typewriter effect would quietly
                             teach the wrong one.

                             Nested one level inside .answer__sentence8, same
                             hand-over technique as every beat before it. -->
                        <div class="answer__sentence9">
                          <p class="answer__reveal-kicker">
                            Each new piece becomes context for the next.
                          </p>
                          <p class="answer__reveal-text">Again. And again.</p>

                          <p class="answer__realise">That's how an answer takes shape.</p>

                          <!-- The way onward. -->
                          <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-understand>
                            So what just happened? <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                          </button>

                          <!-- ====================================================
                               SCENE 11 — the end of Card 2.
                               ====================================================
                               No new mechanism, no new concept: the card closes by
                               handing the reader back the moment it opened on.
                               "AI answered you. In seconds." is Scene 1's headline
                               verbatim, set quietly in the right column this time —
                               the same words, now meaning something they didn't at
                               the start, which is the whole point of the card.

                               Nested one level inside .answer__sentence9, same
                               hand-over technique as every beat before it. -->
                          <div class="answer__sentence10">
                            <p class="answer__echo">
                              <span class="answer__echo-line">AI answered you.</span>
                              <span class="answer__echo-sub">In seconds.</span>
                            </p>

                            <p class="answer__closing">
                              <span class="answer__closing-a">
                                It didn't pull that answer from a page.
                              </span>
                              <span class="answer__closing-b">It built it, piece by piece.</span>
                            </p>

                            <p class="answer__reveal-text">
                              What it learned, your question, and what came before shaped
                              each next piece.
                            </p>

                            <p class="answer__last">
                              <span class="answer__last-a">You saw an answer.</span>
                              <span class="answer__last-b">Now you can see how it took shape.</span>
                            </p>

                            <!-- The end of the card: the only way on is round
                                 again, so this control returns the story to its
                                 opening rather than pointing anywhere new. -->
                            <button type="button" class="answer__turn" data-restart>
                              Replay <span aria-hidden="true">↻</span>
                            </button>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

